# Test step comments

## Motivation

Every test must begin its body with a comment starting with `// Motivation: ...`
and explaining why the test exists.
Place it before any declarations and before the first numbered step, separated
from that step by a blank line.

Motivation comments may span multiple consecutive `//` lines. If a line would
exceed 80 characters, including indentation and the comment prefix, wrap it at
word boundaries so each line stays within that limit. Use `Motivation:` only
on the first line.

```ts
// Motivation: inspection failures must preserve route ownership so a
// temporary Docker outage cannot revoke a live client's access.
```

Describe the behavior or regression the test protects against and why it needs
coverage at this component's level. Do not merely repeat the test name or list
its steps. When adding or editing a test, ensure this comment is present.

## Steps

Each test should be represented as a number of steps.

Each step is one visually separate block:

```pseudocode
// N. what this block does
<declarations of shared objects>
{
    <step body>
}
```

`<declarations of shared objects>` are objects that step N uses for the first
time and that later steps also use. Initialize them there if they must already
exist (for example, an empty collection that the step fills).

`<step body>` may also declare objects that only this step uses.

A loop or a condition is already that block: the body of `for`, `while`, or
`if` is the step body.

When you add or edit a test, write the steps you touch in this shape. Leave
untouched steps as they are.

## Where to declare names

- A name read only inside the step is declared inside the block.
- A name that a later step reads is declared right after the comment of the
  step that first uses it (before the block), and assigned inside that block
  when the comment names that assignment. Initialize it there when the name
  must already exist, such as an empty collection the loop fills.
- Declarations go after the step comment, never above it.

```ts
// 1. player logins
let player;
{
    player = await login("spy007", "iamspy");
}

// 2. administrator spawns a new ship
{
    const spawned = expectOk(await spawner.spawn_ship(...), "spawn ship");
    expect(spawned.owner).toBe(player.id);
}
```

```cpp
// 2. connect and open a commutator session
client::ClientCommutatorPtr pCommutator;
{
  pCommutator = openCommutator();
  ASSERT_TRUE(pCommutator);
}

// 3. game is listed among attached modules
{
  client::ModulesList attached;
  ASSERT_TRUE(pCommutator->getAttachedModulesList(attached));
  ASSERT_EQ(1u, attached.size());
}
```

## Shared objects that need initialization

If several steps share an object and the object needs initialization
(construction with arguments, configuration, attaching to other objects), create
a dedicated initialization step for it. Do not mix its initialization with the
action of a later step.

```cpp
// 1. create an admin socket attached to the terminal
std::shared_ptr<network::AdminSocket> server;
{
  server = std::make_shared<network::AdminSocket>(io, directory);
  server->attachToTerminal(terminal);
}

// 2. only the configured client path reaches the terminal
{
  Local::socket peer(io, Local::endpoint(path("admin.client")));
  peer.send_to(boost::asio::buffer("command", 7),
               Local::endpoint(path("admin.server")));
}
```

## Numbering

- Top-level steps: `1.`, `2.`, `3.`, …
- A loop or condition is one step **X**. The comment is placed **before** it.
- Actions inside the body: `X.1`, `X.2`, …, each in its own block.
- Nested loops use the same pattern (`X.1` before the inner loop, `X.1.1`
  inside).
- A loop or condition body that is a single action stays one step. The outer
  `X` is enough.

```ts
// 1. spawn ships
const spawnedShips = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 100; i += 1) {
    spawnedShips.push(expectOk(await spawner.spawn_ship(...), `spawn ${i}`));
}

// 2. look up spawned ships and check positions
for (const spawned of spawnedShips) {
    let found;
    // 2.1 get the ship via manipulator
    {
        found = expectOk(await manipulator.get_object(...), "get ship");
    }

    // 2.2 check predicted position matches
    {
        expect(almostEqualPosition(found, spawned)).toBe(true);
    }
}
```

## Blank lines

Put a blank line **before** a step when it follows another step of the **same**
level (`2` after `1`, `1.2` after `1.1`). The blank line goes before that
step’s comment (its declarations follow the comment).

A parent step and its first nested step stay on adjacent lines (`1`, then
`1.1` inside the loop). Those are different levels.
